.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt NETUP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm netup
.Nd bring up interfaces from the network configuration file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Ar interface ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads
.Pa /etc/network/interfaces
in the
.Ic ifup
format and configures the selected interfaces.
With no operands or with
.Fl a ,
it includes every interface named by an
.Ic auto
or
.Ic allow-hotplug
line.
An interface named on the command line needs neither declaration.
The file is read in two passes through one open stream.
A read or seek error aborts configuration before any interface is changed.
.Pp
The supported declarations are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic auto Ar name
Include the interface when no operands or
.Fl a
are given.
.It Ic allow-hotplug Ar name
Include the interface in the same way as
.Ic auto .
.It Ic iface Ar name Ic inet dhcp
Run
.Xr dhcpc 8
for the interface.
The client stays in the foreground until its first discovery round
has completed.
.It Ic iface Ar name Ic inet static
Apply a static configuration.
The stanza requires
.Ic address
and
.Ic netmask
entries; a
.Ic gateway
entry is optional.
.It Ic iface Ar name Ic inet6 auto
Turn on
.Va accept_ra
and
.Va autoconf
of the interface in
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf ,
so that it takes the addresses and routes its routers advertise.
They are on unless a program turned them off: IPv6 needs no stanza.
.It Ic iface Ar name Ic inet6 static
Add the address of an
.Ic address
entry, written
.Ar ADDR Ns / Ns Ar LEN
or with its length in a
.Ic netmask
entry, and, with a
.Ic gateway
entry, a default route through that router at metric 1024, beside what
the routers advertise.
An address or route the interface has already is taken as done.
.It Ic iface Ar name Ic inet6 manual
Do nothing, as does
.Ic inet6 loopback .
.El
.Pp
An interface may have an
.Ic inet
stanza, an
.Ic inet6
stanza or both, each applied on its own; another
.Ic inet6
method, or an entry whose address does not parse, is reported and that
stanza alone is skipped.
.Pp
A static stanza is validated in full before any
.Xr ioctl 2
changes the interface.
Static configuration fails if the DHCP client's PID file identifies a
running process, contains no valid positive process ID, or cannot be read.
.Pp
Before any interface,
.Nm
puts IPv6's stable secret in force as
.Xr init 8
does, from
.Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret ,
making it on the first run that finds none, so that the addresses a
router's prefix gives stay the same from one boot to the next.
This image has no DHCPv6 client.
.Pp
On a boot with
.Ql rach2.ip=none ,
init-metal performs the same configuration job.
On os-real, BusyBox's
.Ic ifup
performs this job.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/network/interfaces
.It Pa /etc/network/interfaces
Interface declarations and DHCP or static stanzas.
.It Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret
IPv6's stable secret, an address's text, mode 0600.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl netup -a
The
.Ql tests/run netif
suite exercises DHCP and static stanzas.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
An unknown option produces
.Ql "usage: netup [-a] [interface ...]" .
A DHCP client that stops before it reaches the background produces
.Ql "netup: INTERFACE: dhcpc exited with status N before reaching the background"
or
.Ql "netup: INTERFACE: dhcpc was killed by signal N before reaching the background" ;
such a client may have left its address on the interface.
An
.Ic inet6 static
stanza with no address or no prefix length, or with an address or
gateway that is ::, ::1, a group or a mapped IPv4 address, produces
.Ql "netup: INTERFACE: REASON"
and changes nothing.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr ipv6 7 ,
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr dhcpc 8 ,
.Xr init 8
